    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1012812\">Philipp Weisang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/Obtain.20existential.20witness.20in.20a.20definition/near/578733883\">ha dicho</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I think it might be possible and even cleaner in terms of lean to do it with quotients. I would rather stick close to the presentation in the book I'm using though and they define left quotients in exactly the way I have.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>One often defines a quotient in terms of equivalence classes. But in the categorical sense this is somewhat of a faux-pas. Quotients can be fully characterized by a certain universal property, and it shouldn't matter whether you treat them as the set of all possible representatives, or as some opaque type in a theorem prover.</p>",
        "id": 579583848,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1773617106
    }
]